The core of differential privacy is the management of "privacy loss," a concept that quantifies the increased risk to an individual's privacy from their data being included in a dataset. The most fundamental metric in this regard is the **privacy budget**, often denoted by the Greek letter epsilon (

). A smaller 

 value corresponds to a lower privacy budget, which in turn means less privacy loss and stronger privacy guarantees. The privacy budget is not just a theoretical concept; it is a practical parameter that is set by the data curator to control the level of privacy. For example, in the context of deep learning, a general privacy accounting method called the Wasserstein accountant has been proposed to keep track of the privacy budget, ensuring that it remains stable and is not overestimated, which can be a problem with other methods. Another key aspect of privacy loss is the concept of **adaptive metrics**, which aim to provide a more nuanced approach to privacy and accuracy. Instead of a single, fixed privacy budget for all queries, adaptive metrics can adjust the level of privacy based on the risk associated with each specific query. This allows for a more efficient use of the privacy budget, providing stronger protection for sensitive queries while allowing for greater accuracy on less risky ones. These adaptive metrics can be used to improve the calibration of security and accuracy in differential privacy models. The choice of the privacy budget, or the use of adaptive metrics, directly impacts the trade-off between privacy and utility. A lower privacy budget (stronger privacy) will generally lead to more noise being added to the data, which in turn will reduce its utility. Conversely, a higher privacy budget (weaker privacy) will result in less noise and higher utility. The selection of an appropriate privacy budget is therefore a critical decision that depends on the specific requirements of the application, including the sensitivity of the data and the desired level of accuracy.
